{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VHSSkzPQNUAymbF9PzTon3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the issue clearly. Looking at lines 146-163:\n\n```rust\n// Compute threads_per_block from max_num_y\nlet max_num_y = traces.iter().map(|t| t.num_y).max().unwrap_or(0);\nlet threads_per_block = max_num_y.min(MAX_THREADS_PER_BLOCK);\n\n// Build block_ctxs and air_offsets\nfor (local_air, t) in traces.iter().enumerate() {\n\n\n```\n\nThe code calculates `threads_per_block` based on the maximum `num_y` across all traces, then for each trace it divides the work into blocks with that fixed thread count. For traces with small `num_y` values, this means most threads sit idle—if the max is 512 but a particular trace only needs 4 threads, we're wasting 99.2% of the block's capacity.\n\nLooking at the performance data across different APC levels, I see the problem clearly: at APC 300 where traces are small (num_y of 1-16), the GPU is mostly idle, while at APC 0 with larger traces, thread utilization is much better. The solution is to group small traces together so they share blocks efficiently instead of each getting their own mostly-empty block.\n\nMy approach is to partition traces into small and large categories, keep the existing one-block-per-trace strategy for large traces, and then pack multiple small traces into shared blocks where each thread handles one (y_int, trace_idx) pair.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the grid dimensions. The grid is structured as `dim3(num_blocks, num_x)` where `num_x` is the spline degree (2 or 3), so at APC 300 with 623 traces that are mostly small, I'd have 623 blocks × 3 = 1869 total grid size, which explains the severe underutilization.\n\nWith 128 SMs, that's only about 15 waves, and since each block has 512 threads but most are idle, I'm only using 2-5% of compute capacity per wave. If I coalesce small traces—say 100 traces with num_y=4 each—they could fit i