ly, which could save 15-30ms on MLE Rounds with moderate complexity. The second idea is to parallelize multiple independent commitments within a segment using separate CUDA streams instead of building them sequentially.\n\nFor the third approach, I'm thinking about pre-computing the trace classification and descriptors for all rounds upfront, then uploading everything to the GPU in one batch before the MLE loop starts—this eliminates the per-round CPU overhead and repeated host-to-device transfers, saving 15-25ms with straightforward precomputation logic. The fourth idea involves fusing multiple rounds of the fractional sumcheck kernel together rather than launching them separately. GKR fractional sumcheck round is a separate kernel launch\n- Fuse N consecutive rounds into one kernel that computes + processes internally\n- Eliminates per-round kernel launch + D2H overhead\n- Expected impact: 20-40ms on LogUp GKR (from 141 launches → ~14 launches)\n- Probability of success: Low-Medium (CUDA complexity, may hit registers/memory limits)\n- Complexity: Complex (requires GPU-side transcript, changes protocol flow)\n\n**Idea 5: Multi-stream LogUp GKR across segments**\n- Currently, segments are processed sequentially within LogUp GKR\n- If two segments are independent (they are after GKR input eval), overlap their fractional sumcheck processing\n- Expected impact: 80-120ms on LogUp GKR (process both segments in parallel)\n- Probability of success: Low-Medium (segments share transcript state, creating dependencies)\n- Complexity: Complex (need to manage inter-segment dependencies)\n\nActually, I need to reconsider whether segments can truly be parallelized. Looking at the code flow, each segment's GKR produces proof elements that feed into the transcript, which then affects the challenges for subsequent segments. So they're inherently sequential in the GKR phase, not independent.\n\n**Idea 5 (revised): Overlap LogUp GKR with Trace Commit across segments**\n- Could pipeline