{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013ZLxiJAEwspxnCuJR1UYGe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018DCsbefqnmhL9HXCFdVTTr","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval\n\nMLE Rounds at APC 300 (159ms) scales negatively vs APC 0 (112ms, ratio 0.70x) despite 2.3x fewer cells, because the batch MLE evaluation kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one full 128-thread block per trace — wasting >75% of threads when `num_y` is small (1–32). At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances (many small APC-generated AIRs), most traces have tiny `num_y`, and the problem worsens each MLE round as `num_y` halves. A warp-per-trace evaluation kernel for small traces (num_y ≤ 32) packs 4 traces into one 128-thread block with warp-level reduction, dramatically reducing block count and improving GPU utilization.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1054,"cache_read_input_tokens":155425,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1054},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6086bd80-4e0d-45db-9a25-2370973b0822","uuid":"ea8ea712-c311-4be4-8fa5-18ecddafad40"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018DCsbefqnmhL9HXCFdVTTr","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6086bd80-4e0d-45db-9a25-2370973b0822","uuid":"8f5c52aa-0d8e-4147-936d-4eae077fb3f8","timestamp":"2026-04-14T21:02:20.896Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval\n\nMLE Rounds at APC 300 (159ms) scales negatively vs APC 0 (112ms, ratio 0.70x) despite 2.3x fewer cells, because the batch MLE evaluation kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one full 128-thread block per trace — wasting >75% of threads when `num_y` is small (1–32). At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances (many small APC-generated AIRs), most traces have tiny `num_y`, and the problem worsens each MLE round as `num_y` halves. A warp-per-trace evaluation kernel for small traces (num_y ≤ 32) packs 4 traces into one 128-thread block with warp-level reduction, dramatically reducing block count and improving GPU utilization.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-14-2000-matrix-base-ptr-interpolation","+# 2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval"," ","-Replace the flat per-column pointer array (~106K entries at APC 300) with per-matrix base pointers (~2K entries) in the batched interpolation kernel used during MLE rounds. Currently, each MLE round rebuilds a `Vec<*const EF>` of all column GPU addresses by iterating O(#total_columns) on the CPU (~1.6ms per round), then uploads it H2D (850KB). By passing per-matrix base pointers and widths instead, the kernel computes column addresses internally via `base + col * height`, reducing the CPU-side collection from O(106K) to O(2K) and the H2D from 850KB to ~40KB per round.","+MLE Rounds at APC 300 (159ms) scales negatively vs APC 0 (112ms, ratio 0.70x) despite 2.3x fewer cells, because the batch MLE evaluation kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one full 128-thread block per trace — wasting >75% of threads when `num_y` is small (1–32). At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances (many small APC-generated AIRs), most traces have tiny `num_y`, and the problem worsens each MLE round as `num_y` halves. A warp-per-trace evaluation kernel for small traces (num_y ≤ 32) packs 4 traces into one 128-thread block with warp-level reduction, dramatically reducing block count and improving GPU utilization."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-14-2000-matrix-base-ptr-interpolation\n\nReplace the flat per-column pointer array (~106K entries at APC 300) with per-matrix base pointers (~2K entries) in the batched interpolation kernel used during MLE rounds. Currently, each MLE round rebuilds a `Vec<*const EF>` of all column GPU addresses by iterating O(#total_columns) on the CPU (~1.6ms per round), then uploads it H2D (850KB). By passing per-matrix base pointers and widths instead, the kernel computes column addresses internally via `base + col * height`, reducing the CPU-side collection from O(106K) to O(2K) and the H2D from 850KB to ~40KB per round.\n"}}
