{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ucn17F3je193iH7eCcVTFZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAM9LTqLr3Hcmyqk8a9fnd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Warp-per-trace MLE batch evaluation for small traces\n\n## Goal\n\nImprove MLE Rounds GPU utilization at APC 300 by introducing a warp-per-trace evaluation path for traces with small `num_y`. Currently, the batch MLE kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one or more 128-thread blocks per trace regardless of `num_y`. Traces with `num_y` ≤ 32 waste ≥75% of threads. A dedicated warp-per-trace kernel packs 4 small traces per 128-thread block, directly producing per-trace sums via warp-level reduction and eliminating the secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel for the small-trace partition.\n\nTarget: reduce MLE Rounds at APC 300 from 159ms toward ~120–135ms (saving 25–40ms), improving the APC 0→300 scaling from 0.70x toward ~0.86–0.93x.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Zerocheck batch MLE\n\n1. **Rust builder** (`batch_mle.rs:121–218`, `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n   - Computes `threads_per_block = max_num_y.min(128)` across ALL traces (line 147).\n   - Builds `BlockCtx` array: one entry per block per trace. For a trace with `num_y = 4` and `threads_per_block = 128`, it gets 1 block where only 4/128 = 3.1% of threads are active.\n   - Allocates per-trace `ZerocheckCtx` with intermediates buffers.\n   - Uploads block_ctxs, zc_ctxs, air_offsets to device.\n\n2. **CUDA kernel** (`batch_mle.cu:109–204`, `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block)`.\n   - Each block reads its `BlockCtx` → gets `air_idx` and `local_block_idx_x`.\n   - Thread computes `y_int = threadIdx.x + local_block_idx_x * blockDim.x`. Guard: `active_thread = (y_int < num_y)`.\n   - Active threads evaluate the constraint DAG at `(x_int, y_int)`, multiply by `eq_xi[y_int]`, accumulate sum.\n   - `block_reduce_sum` (sumcheck.cuh:160–185): warp reduce → shared memory → first-warp reduce.\n   - Thread 0 writes to `tmp_sums_buffer[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`.\n\n3. **Secondary reduction** (`batch_mle.cu:400–410`, `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`):\n   - Grid: `(num_airs, num_x)`. Reduces per-block partial sums to per-air output.\n   - For a trace with 1 block, this is a no-op copy. Still costs one block dispatch.\n\n### Logup batch MLE\n\nIdentical structure (`batch_mle.rs:268–408`, `batch_mle.cu:206–448`). The kernel computes `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt), with two sequential `block_reduce_sum` calls plus `__syncthreads` between them.\n\n### Where time is wasted at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 with 623 AIR instances across 2 segments:\n- `max_num_y` is large (up to 2^20) → `threads_per_block = 128` always.\n- In early MLE rounds: many traces have `num_y` = 1–32 (small APC-generated AIRs, plus smaller original AIRs).\n- In later MLE rounds: `num_y` halves each round, so even originally-large traces eventually become \"small.\"\n- Each small trace gets its own 128-thread block. With 500+ small traces per launch, this produces 1500+ blocks (×3 x-points) with <25% average thread utilization.\n- The secondary reduction kernel also launches 1 block per small trace, mostly doing trivial single-element \"reductions.\"\n\nnsight kernel profile:\n- `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`: 24 instances, 27ms total, 1.1ms avg.\n- `logup_batch_mle_kernel`: 45 instances, 21ms total, 0.46ms avg.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums<true>`: 797 instances, 7.8ms total.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` (logup variant via FracExt): similar.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace zerocheck evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel(\n    FpExt *__restrict__ output,           // [num_traces * num_x], direct final output\n    const ZerocheckCtx *__restrict__ d_zc_ctxs,\n    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids,  // [num_warps] maps warp → trace local index\n    const FpExt *__restrict__ d_lambda_pows,\n    size_t lambda_len,\n    uint32_t num_traces\n) {\n    uint32_t warp_id_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t num_x = gridDim.y;\n    uint32_t x_int = blockIdx.y;\n\n    if (warp_id_global >= num_traces) return;\n    uint32_t local_air = trace_ids[warp_id_global];\n    ZerocheckCtx zc_ctx = d_zc_ctxs[local_air];\n\n    uint32_t y_int = lane;\n    bool active = (y_int < zc_ctx.num_y);\n\n    FpExt sum(Fp::zero());\n    if (active) {\n        // Same evaluation logic as existing kernel (lines 128–197 of batch_mle.cu)\n        uint32_t height = num_x * zc_ctx.num_y;\n        uint32_t row = x_int * zc_ctx.num_y + y_int;\n        // ... [DAG evaluation, eq_xi multiply — identical to existing kernel body]\n    }\n\n    // Warp-level reduction only (no shared memory, no block_reduce_sum)\n    sum = warp_reduce_sum(sum);\n\n    if (lane == 0) {\n        output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey differences from existing kernel:\n- **One warp per trace** (not one block per trace). 4 warps per 128-thread block → 4 traces per block.\n- **`warp_reduce_sum` only** — no shared memory, no `__syncthreads`, no inter-warp reduction.\n- **Direct output** — writes to final `output` buffer, no `tmp_sums_buffer` or secondary reduction needed.\n- **`trace_ids` array** maps warp index → ZerocheckCtx index, allowing the same `d_zc_ctxs` array as the large-trace kernel.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle`:\n- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / warps_per_block), num_x)`, Block: `(128)` (= 4 warps).\n- `warps_per_block = 128 / WARP_SIZE = 4`.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_logup_batch_mle_kernel` — same pattern as Change 1 but with `FracExt` output (numer + denom) and two `warp_reduce_sum` calls:\n\n```cuda\nFpExt numer_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(numer_sum);\nFpExt denom_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(denom_sum);\nif (lane == 0) {\n    output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = {numer_reduced, denom_reduced};\n}\n```\n\nNo `__syncthreads` needed between the two reductions because they operate within a single warp.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_logup_batch_eval_mle`.\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings for new kernels\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers:\n- `warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(output, zc_ctxs, trace_ids, lambda_pows, lambda_len, num_traces, num_x)`\n- `warp_logup_batch_eval_mle(output, logup_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x)`\n\n### Change 4: Partition logic in builders\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n\n```rust\nconst WARP_SIZE: u32 = 32;\n\n// Partition traces into small (num_y <= WARP_SIZE) and large (num_y > WARP_SIZE)\nlet (small_traces, large_traces): (Vec<&TraceCtx>, Vec<&TraceCtx>) =\n    traces.into_iter().partition(|t| t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE);\n```\n\nStore both partitions in the builder. The builder gets new fields:\n```rust\n// Existing fields (now only for large traces):\nd_block_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\nthreads_per_block: u32,\nair_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\n// New fields (for small traces, warp-per-trace path):\nsmall_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,  // maps warp → zc_ctx index\nnum_small_traces: u32,\n```\n\nThe `d_zc_ctxs` / `d_logup_ctxs` arrays contain ALL traces (small + large) indexed by their local position. The large-trace path uses `BlockCtx.air_idx` to index into it; the small-trace path uses `trace_ids[warp_id_global]`.\n\n### Change 5: Two-launch evaluation in builder.evaluate()\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::evaluate` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::evaluate`):\n\n```rust\npub fn evaluate(&self, lambda_pows: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {\n    let total_traces = self.traces.len();\n    if total_traces == 0 {\n        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n    }\n\n    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_traces * num_x as usize);\n\n    // Launch 1: Small traces via warp-per-trace kernel (direct output, no tmp buffer)\n    if self.num_small_traces > 0 {\n        unsafe {\n            warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(\n                &mut output,  // writes to output[0..num_small * num_x]\n                &self.d_zc_ctxs,\n                &self.small_trace_ids,\n                lambda_pows,\n                lambda_pows.len(),\n                self.num_small_traces,\n                num_x,\n            )?;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Launch 2: Large traces via existing block-per-trace kernel\n    if !self.d_block_ctxs.is_empty() {\n        let num_large = self.d_block_ctxs.len(); // blocks, not traces\n        let num_large_airs = self.large_trace_count;\n        // tmp_sums_buffer and secondary reduce, writing to output[num_small * num_x..]\n        // ...existing code with offset into output buffer...\n    }\n\n    Ok(output)\n}\n```\n\nThe output buffer layout: `[small_trace_0_x0, small_trace_0_x1, ..., small_trace_N_x0, ..., large_trace_0_x0, ...]`. The caller reads by trace index, so we maintain a mapping from original trace index → output position. This is handled by ordering the `traces` Vec consistently: small traces first, then large traces.\n\n### Change 6: Adjust callers to handle reordered output\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nThe `trace_indices()` / `trace_info()` iterators already return trace indices in the order they were stored. With the partition, the builder stores small traces first, then large traces. Callers iterate over `trace_indices()` and use the index to write into the correct `zc_out[trace_idx]` or `logup_out[trace_idx]`, so no caller changes are needed as long as the builder maintains the trace-index-to-output-position mapping correctly.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The warp-per-trace kernel must produce identical per-trace sums as the existing block-per-trace kernel. Both evaluate the same DAG over the same data and reduce to a single sum per trace per x-evaluation-point. The warp-level reduction is exact (no atomics, no floating-point reordering) because it uses `warp_reduce_sum` which is the same function used in the first stage of `block_reduce_sum`.\n\n2. **Threshold correctness**: Traces with `num_y > WARP_SIZE` must NOT use the warp path (they'd lose data — lane_id only covers 0..31). The partition at `num_y <= 32` guarantees all active threads fit in one warp.\n\n3. **Intermediates buffers**: Small traces with `buffer_size > 0` still need intermediates. The intermediates allocation uses `height = num_x * num_y`, which is correct regardless of kernel type. The `buffer_stride = height` in the kernel is also correct.\n\n4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large heights → most go to the existing block-per-trace path. The small-trace partition may have 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead from the extra kernel launch.\n\n5. **Memory**: The `small_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_small_traces * 4 bytes` (~2KB at APC 300). The output buffer size is unchanged (one entry per trace per x-point). The `tmp_sums_buffer` is only allocated for large traces, so its size decreases.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n2. Verify correctness: Run `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass.\n3. Measure APC 300:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc300.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc300.json apc300\n   ```\n   Check: MLE Rounds < 135ms, STARK excl trace < 1260ms.\n4. Measure APC 0:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc000.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc000.json apc000\n   ```\n   Check: No regression (MLE Rounds ≤ 115ms, STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).\n5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm reduced block count and improved kernel time for `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below noise threshold): revert.\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 15ms: revert.\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace > 2160ms): revert.\n- Any test failure: revert.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":396,"cache_read_input_tokens":167341,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":396},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6086bd80-4e0d-45db-9a25-2370973b0822","uuid":"1b8700f9-b818-4a2e-91a7-c1538e10c525"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAM9LTqLr3Hcmyqk8a9fnd","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval/plan.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6086bd80-4e0d-45db-9a25-2370973b0822","uuid":"c39bc9e3-74cd-4b99-b2f7-fed082b78764","timestamp":"2026-04-14T21:04:20.471Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Warp-per-trace MLE batch evaluation for small traces\n\n## Goal\n\nImprove MLE Rounds GPU utilization at APC 300 by introducing a warp-per-trace evaluation path for traces with small `num_y`. Currently, the batch MLE kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one or more 128-thread blocks per trace regardless of `num_y`. Traces with `num_y` ≤ 32 waste ≥75% of threads. A dedicated warp-per-trace kernel packs 4 small traces per 128-thread block, directly producing per-trace sums via warp-level reduction and eliminating the secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel for the small-trace partition.\n\nTarget: reduce MLE Rounds at APC 300 from 159ms toward ~120–135ms (saving 25–40ms), improving the APC 0→300 scaling from 0.70x toward ~0.86–0.93x.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Zerocheck batch MLE\n\n1. **Rust builder** (`batch_mle.rs:121–218`, `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n   - Computes `threads_per_block = max_num_y.min(128)` across ALL traces (line 147).\n   - Builds `BlockCtx` array: one entry per block per trace. For a trace with `num_y = 4` and `threads_per_block = 128`, it gets 1 block where only 4/128 = 3.1% of threads are active.\n   - Allocates per-trace `ZerocheckCtx` with intermediates buffers.\n   - Uploads block_ctxs, zc_ctxs, air_offsets to device.\n\n2. **CUDA kernel** (`batch_mle.cu:109–204`, `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block)`.\n   - Each block reads its `BlockCtx` → gets `air_idx` and `local_block_idx_x`.\n   - Thread computes `y_int = threadIdx.x + local_block_idx_x * blockDim.x`. Guard: `active_thread = (y_int < num_y)`.\n   - Active threads evaluate the constraint DAG at `(x_int, y_int)`, multiply by `eq_xi[y_int]`, accumulate sum.\n   - `block_reduce_sum` (sumcheck.cuh:160–185): warp reduce → shared memory → first-warp reduce.\n   - Thread 0 writes to `tmp_sums_buffer[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`.\n\n3. **Secondary reduction** (`batch_mle.cu:400–410`, `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`):\n   - Grid: `(num_airs, num_x)`. Reduces per-block partial sums to per-air output.\n   - For a trace with 1 block, this is a no-op copy. Still costs one block dispatch.\n\n### Logup batch MLE\n\nIdentical structure (`batch_mle.rs:268–408`, `batch_mle.cu:206–448`). The kernel computes `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt), with two sequential `block_reduce_sum` calls plus `__syncthreads` between them.\n\n### Where time is wasted at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 with 623 AIR instances across 2 segments:\n- `max_num_y` is large (up to 2^20) → `threads_per_block = 128` always.\n- In early MLE rounds: many traces have `num_y` = 1–32 (small APC-generated AIRs, plus smaller original AIRs).\n- In later MLE rounds: `num_y` halves each round, so even originally-large traces eventually become \"small.\"\n- Each small trace gets its own 128-thread block. With 500+ small traces per launch, this produces 1500+ blocks (×3 x-points) with <25% average thread utilization.\n- The secondary reduction kernel also launches 1 block per small trace, mostly doing trivial single-element \"reductions.\"\n\nnsight kernel profile:\n- `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`: 24 instances, 27ms total, 1.1ms avg.\n- `logup_batch_mle_kernel`: 45 instances, 21ms total, 0.46ms avg.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums<true>`: 797 instances, 7.8ms total.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` (logup variant via FracExt): similar.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace zerocheck evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel(\n    FpExt *__restrict__ output,           // [num_traces * num_x], direct final output\n    const ZerocheckCtx *__restrict__ d_zc_ctxs,\n    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids,  // [num_warps] maps warp → trace local index\n    const FpExt *__restrict__ d_lambda_pows,\n    size_t lambda_len,\n    uint32_t num_traces\n) {\n    uint32_t warp_id_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t num_x = gridDim.y;\n    uint32_t x_int = blockIdx.y;\n\n    if (warp_id_global >= num_traces) return;\n    uint32_t local_air = trace_ids[warp_id_global];\n    ZerocheckCtx zc_ctx = d_zc_ctxs[local_air];\n\n    uint32_t y_int = lane;\n    bool active = (y_int < zc_ctx.num_y);\n\n    FpExt sum(Fp::zero());\n    if (active) {\n        // Same evaluation logic as existing kernel (lines 128–197 of batch_mle.cu)\n        uint32_t height = num_x * zc_ctx.num_y;\n        uint32_t row = x_int * zc_ctx.num_y + y_int;\n        // ... [DAG evaluation, eq_xi multiply — identical to existing kernel body]\n    }\n\n    // Warp-level reduction only (no shared memory, no block_reduce_sum)\n    sum = warp_reduce_sum(sum);\n\n    if (lane == 0) {\n        output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey differences from existing kernel:\n- **One warp per trace** (not one block per trace). 4 warps per 128-thread block → 4 traces per block.\n- **`warp_reduce_sum` only** — no shared memory, no `__syncthreads`, no inter-warp reduction.\n- **Direct output** — writes to final `output` buffer, no `tmp_sums_buffer` or secondary reduction needed.\n- **`trace_ids` array** maps warp index → ZerocheckCtx index, allowing the same `d_zc_ctxs` array as the large-trace kernel.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle`:\n- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / warps_per_block), num_x)`, Block: `(128)` (= 4 warps).\n- `warps_per_block = 128 / WARP_SIZE = 4`.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_logup_batch_mle_kernel` — same pattern as Change 1 but with `FracExt` output (numer + denom) and two `warp_reduce_sum` calls:\n\n```cuda\nFpExt numer_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(numer_sum);\nFpExt denom_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(denom_sum);\nif (lane == 0) {\n    output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = {numer_reduced, denom_reduced};\n}\n```\n\nNo `__syncthreads` needed between the two reductions because they operate within a single warp.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_logup_batch_eval_mle`.\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings for new kernels\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers:\n- `warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(output, zc_ctxs, trace_ids, lambda_pows, lambda_len, num_traces, num_x)`\n- `warp_logup_batch_eval_mle(output, logup_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x)`\n\n### Change 4: Partition logic in builders\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n\n```rust\nconst WARP_SIZE: u32 = 32;\n\n// Partition traces into small (num_y <= WARP_SIZE) and large (num_y > WARP_SIZE)\nlet (small_traces, large_traces): (Vec<&TraceCtx>, Vec<&TraceCtx>) =\n    traces.into_iter().partition(|t| t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE);\n```\n\nStore both partitions in the builder. The builder gets new fields:\n```rust\n// Existing fields (now only for large traces):\nd_block_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\nthreads_per_block: u32,\nair_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\n// New fields (for small traces, warp-per-trace path):\nsmall_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,  // maps warp → zc_ctx index\nnum_small_traces: u32,\n```\n\nThe `d_zc_ctxs` / `d_logup_ctxs` arrays contain ALL traces (small + large) indexed by their local position. The large-trace path uses `BlockCtx.air_idx` to index into it; the small-trace path uses `trace_ids[warp_id_global]`.\n\n### Change 5: Two-launch evaluation in builder.evaluate()\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::evaluate` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::evaluate`):\n\n```rust\npub fn evaluate(&self, lambda_pows: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {\n    let total_traces = self.traces.len();\n    if total_traces == 0 {\n        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n    }\n\n    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_traces * num_x as usize);\n\n    // Launch 1: Small traces via warp-per-trace kernel (direct output, no tmp buffer)\n    if self.num_small_traces > 0 {\n        unsafe {\n            warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(\n                &mut output,  // writes to output[0..num_small * num_x]\n                &self.d_zc_ctxs,\n                &self.small_trace_ids,\n                lambda_pows,\n                lambda_pows.len(),\n                self.num_small_traces,\n                num_x,\n            )?;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Launch 2: Large traces via existing block-per-trace kernel\n    if !self.d_block_ctxs.is_empty() {\n        let num_large = self.d_block_ctxs.len(); // blocks, not traces\n        let num_large_airs = self.large_trace_count;\n        // tmp_sums_buffer and secondary reduce, writing to output[num_small * num_x..]\n        // ...existing code with offset into output buffer...\n    }\n\n    Ok(output)\n}\n```\n\nThe output buffer layout: `[small_trace_0_x0, small_trace_0_x1, ..., small_trace_N_x0, ..., large_trace_0_x0, ...]`. The caller reads by trace index, so we maintain a mapping from original trace index → output position. This is handled by ordering the `traces` Vec consistently: small traces first, then large traces.\n\n### Change 6: Adjust callers to handle reordered output\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nThe `trace_indices()` / `trace_info()` iterators already return trace indices in the order they were stored. With the partition, the builder stores small traces first, then large traces. Callers iterate over `trace_indices()` and use the index to write into the correct `zc_out[trace_idx]` or `logup_out[trace_idx]`, so no caller changes are needed as long as the builder maintains the trace-index-to-output-position mapping correctly.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The warp-per-trace kernel must produce identical per-trace sums as the existing block-per-trace kernel. Both evaluate the same DAG over the same data and reduce to a single sum per trace per x-evaluation-point. The warp-level reduction is exact (no atomics, no floating-point reordering) because it uses `warp_reduce_sum` which is the same function used in the first stage of `block_reduce_sum`.\n\n2. **Threshold correctness**: Traces with `num_y > WARP_SIZE` must NOT use the warp path (they'd lose data — lane_id only covers 0..31). The partition at `num_y <= 32` guarantees all active threads fit in one warp.\n\n3. **Intermediates buffers**: Small traces with `buffer_size > 0` still need intermediates. The intermediates allocation uses `height = num_x * num_y`, which is correct regardless of kernel type. The `buffer_stride = height` in the kernel is also correct.\n\n4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large heights → most go to the existing block-per-trace path. The small-trace partition may have 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead from the extra kernel launch.\n\n5. **Memory**: The `small_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_small_traces * 4 bytes` (~2KB at APC 300). The output buffer size is unchanged (one entry per trace per x-point). The `tmp_sums_buffer` is only allocated for large traces, so its size decreases.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n2. Verify correctness: Run `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass.\n3. Measure APC 300:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc300.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc300.json apc300\n   ```\n   Check: MLE Rounds < 135ms, STARK excl trace < 1260ms.\n4. Measure APC 0:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc000.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc000.json apc000\n   ```\n   Check: No regression (MLE Rounds ≤ 115ms, STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).\n5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm reduced block count and improved kernel time for `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below noise threshold): revert.\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 15ms: revert.\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace > 2160ms): revert.\n- Any test failure: revert.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
