ich is the cleanest change and directly fixes the thread underutilization.\n\nThough honestly, 5-10ms might fall below the rollback threshold of around 10ms for MLE Rounds. I need at least 15ms of savings to have a meaningful signal. Maybe combining a couple of these approaches is the right call.\n\nActually, I think the warp-per-trace strategy for the monomial kernel is the way to go — it's cleaner than I initially thought. Instead of one thread per monomial with one block per y value, I'd have each thread evaluate all monomials for a single y value, with one warp handling one trace and multiple traces sharing a block. For a small trace with 10 monomials and 8 y values, this drops from 2048 threads doing 80 useful operations down to something far more efficient.\n\nThe beauty of this approach is that I don't need intermediate buffers or complex DAG traversal — monomials are independent, so it's just a straightforward loop over them per thread.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1806,"cache_read_input_tokens":182102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1806},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6086bd80-4e0d-45db-9a25-2370973b0822","uuid":"82fc2e6d-09ae-4be9-b213-133b40465243"}
