k_monomial_par_y_kernel`.\n- **high_dag_traces** (num_y > 512, high monomial ratio): `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder` → launches `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`.\n\nFor logup, analogous: `LogupMonomialBatch` (num_y ≤ 512), `LogupMleBatchBuilder` (num_y > 512).\n\nAt APC 300 with 623 small AIR instances: the vast majority have num_y ≤ 512 in early rounds, and ALL traces transition to late_eval (num_y=1) as MLE rounds progress. The `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` and `LogupMonomialBatch` handle most of the work.\n\n### zerocheck_monomial_kernel (`batch_mle_monomial.cu:102–151`)\n\nGrid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block=256)`.\n\nBlock assignment (Rust, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:152–168`):\n```\nmono_blocks = ceil(num_monomials / 256)\ntotal_blocks = mono_blocks * num_y   // one set of mono_blocks per y-value\n```\n\nKernel logic:\n- Decodes `y_int = local_block_idx_x / mono_blocks` and `mono_block = local_block_idx_x % mono_blocks`.\n- Thread `m = mono_block * 256 + threadIdx.x` evaluates monomial `m` if `m < num_monomials`.\n- Evaluates product of variables for that monomial, multiplies by `lambda_combinations[m]`.\n- `block_reduce_sum` sums across all threads → per-(y_int, x_int) partial sum.\n- Thread 0 multiplies by `eq_xi[y_int]` and writes to `tmp_sums[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`.\n\nSecondary reduction: `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduces across mono_blocks for same (air, y_int).\n\n**Waste at APC 300**: A typical APC AIR has `num_monomials ≈ 5–15`. With `threads_per_block = 256`:\n- `mono_blocks = 1` (ceil(15/256) = 1)\n- 256 threads per block, 15 active → 5.9% utilization\n- Per trace: `num_y` blocks of 256 threads each, totaling `num_y × 256` threads with `num_y × 15` useful FMAs\n\nnsight profile: `zerocheck_monomial_kernel`: 178 instances, 17ms total, 0.09ms avg.\n\n### LogupMonomialBatch (`batch_mle_monomial.rs:588–793`)\n\nSame pattern. `THREADS_PER_BLOCK_LOGUP = 128`. Block assignment differs slightly (accounts for both numerator and denominator interactions). Same utilization problem.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace monomial evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n// Warp-per-trace: each warp handles one trace.\n// Thread = one y-value. Each thread loops over ALL monomials for its y-value.\n// Constraint: num_y <= WARP_SIZE and num_monomials <= warp_mono_limit (e.g. 32).\n__global__ void warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel(\n    FpExt *__restrict__ output,           // [num_traces * num_x], direct final output\n    const MonomialAirCtx *__restrict__ air_ctxs,\n    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids, // [num_warps] → air_ctxs index\n    uint32_t num_traces\n) {\n    constexpr uint32_t WARP = 32;\n    uint32_t warp_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP;\n    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP;\n    uint32_t num_x = gridDim.y;\n    uint32_t x_int = blockIdx.y;\n\n    if (warp_global >= num_traces) return;\n    MonomialAirCtx actx = air_ctxs[trace_ids[warp_global]];\n\n    uint32_t y_int = lane;\n    bool active = (y_int < actx.num_y);\n\n    FpExt sum(Fp::zero());\n    if (active) {\n        uint32_t height = num_x * actx.num_y;\n        uint32_t row = x_int * actx.num_y + y_int;\n\n        // Loop over ALL monomials (small count, so serial is fine)\n        for (uint32_t m = 0; m < actx.num_monomials; ++m) {\n            MonomialHeader hdr = actx.d_headers[m];\n            FpExt product(Fp::one());\n            for (uint16_t v = 0; v < hdr.num_vars; ++v) {\n                PackedVar var = actx.d_variables[hdr.var_offset + v];\n                product *= eval_variable(var, row, actx.eval_ctx, height);\n            }\n            sum += product * actx.d_lambda_combinations[m];\n        }\n        sum *= actx.d_eq_xi[y_int];\n    }\n\n    // Warp reduction: sum across y-values in this trace\n    sum = warp_reduce_sum(sum);\n\n    if (lane == 0) {\n        output[warp_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey design choices:\n- **Parallelism axis flipped**: threads map to y-values (not monomials). Each thread serially evaluates all monomials — this is correct because `num_monomials ≤ 32`, so the serial loop is short.\n- **Direct output**: writes to final output buffer. No `tmp_sums` intermediary, no secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` launch.\n- **Warp reduction**: `warp_reduce_sum` sums across y-values within the trace. No shared memory needed.\n- **4 warps per block** (128 threads): 4 traces processed per block.\n- **`trace_ids` indirection**: maps warp index → `air_ctxs` slot, allowing the same `air_ctxs` array to serve both the warp kernel (small traces) and the existing kernel (large traces).\n\nAdd C launcher `_warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched`:\n- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / 4), num_x)`, Block: `(128)`.\n- No tmp_sums allocation, no secondary reduce launch.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup monomial evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_logup_monomial_kernel` — same warp-per-trace pattern, but accumulates both `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt output). Two `warp_reduce_sum` calls (no `__syncthreads` needed — single warp).\n\nThe logup monomial kernel (`logup_monomial_kernel`, `batch_mle_monomial.cu`) iterates over interactions and evaluates numerator/denominator expressions. The warp kernel maintains the same logic but with each thread covering one y-value and looping over interactions.\n\nAdd C launcher `_warp_logup_monomial_batched`.\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers:\n- `warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched(output, air_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x) -> Result`\n- `warp_logup_monomial_batched(output, logup_air_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x) -> Result`\n\n### Change 4: Partition in ZerocheckMonomialBatch\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new`:\n\n```rust\nconst WARP_SIZE: u32 = 32;\n\n// Partition traces: \"warp-eligible\" if num_y <= WARP_SIZE AND num_monomials <= WARP_SIZE\nlet (warp_traces, block_traces): (Vec<_>, Vec<_>) = traces\n    .into_iter()\n    .enumerate()\n    .partition(|(_, t)| {\n        let nm = pk.per_air[t.air_idx].other_data.zerocheck_monomials\n            .as_ref().unwrap().num_monomials;\n        t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE && nm <= WARP_SIZE\n    });\n```\n\nNew builder fields:\n```rust\n// Warp-path fields:\nwarp_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,   // maps warp → air_ctxs index\nnum_warp_traces: u32,\n// Block-path fields (existing):\nblock_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\nblock_air_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\nnum_block_traces: u32,\n// Shared:\nair_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<MonomialAirCtx>,  // all traces, indexed by local position\n```\n\nThe `air_ctxs` array contains ALL traces (warp + block), with warp-eligible traces first. The `warp_trace_ids` maps warp index → position in `air_ctxs`. The `block_ctxs` and `block_air_offsets` only reference block-eligible traces via `BlockCtx.air_idx` pointing into the same `air_ctxs` array (with appropriate offset).\n\n### Change 5: Two-launch evaluate\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate`:\n\n```rust\npub fn evaluate(&self, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {\n    let total = self.traces.len();\n    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total * num_x as usize);\n\n    // Launch 1: warp-per-trace for small traces → writes to output[0..num_warp * num_x]\n    if self.num_warp_traces > 0 {\n        unsafe {\n            warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched(\n                &mut output, &self.air_ctxs, &self.warp_trace_ids,\n                self.num_warp_traces, num_x,\n            )?;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Launch 2: existing block kernel for remaining traces\n    if self.num_block_traces > 0 {\n        let offset = self.num_warp_traces as usize * num_x as usize;\n        // Allocate tmp_sums for block path\n        let num_blocks = self.block_ctxs.len();\n        let mut tmp_sums = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_blocks * num_x as usize);\n        unsafe {\n            zerocheck_monomial_batched(\n                &mut tmp_sums,\n                output.as_mut_ptr().add(offset), // write after warp output\n                &self.block_ctxs, &self.air_ctxs,\n                &self.block_air_offsets,\n                num_blocks as u32, num_x,\n                self.num_block_traces, THREADS_PER_BLOCK,\n            )?;\n        }\n    }\n\n    Ok(output)\n}\n```\n\nThe output layout: warp-eligible traces first (contiguous), then block-eligible traces. `trace_indices()` returns indices in the same order (warp first, block second).\n\n### Change 6: Apply same partition to LogupMonomialBatch\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`\n\nSame pattern for `LogupMonomialBatch::new` and `evaluate`, using `warp_logup_monomial_batched`. The logup monomial kernel has `THREADS_PER_BLOCK_LOGUP = 128`, similar underutilization.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The warp kernel evaluates the same monomials × lambda_combinations for the same (x_int, y_int) pairs, producing the same per-trace sums. The serial monomial loop in the warp kernel is mathematically equivalent to the parallel-monomial approach in the block kernel, because addition is commutative and associative over the field. `warp_reduce_sum` over y-values produces the same result as the block kernel's `block_reduce_sum` over mono-blocks followed by `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` over (y_int × mono_blocks).\n\n2. **Threshold safety**: Only traces with `num_y <= WARP_SIZE=32` AND `num_monomials <= WARP_SIZE=32` use the warp path. The `num_y` guard ensures no y-values are missed (thread with `lane >= num_y` contributes zero). The `num_monomials` bound ensures the serial loop is short enough that per-thread work doesn't become a bottleneck.\n\n3. **No intermediates needed**: The monomial kernel does NOT use intermediates buffers (unlike the DAG batch kernel). Each monomial is evaluated independently from trace data. This means the warp kernel needs no intermediates allocation at all.\n\n4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large num_y (>32) → they go to the block kernel path. The warp partition has 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead.\n\n5. **Memory**: The `warp_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_warp_traces * 4` bytes (≈2KB). The `tmp_sums` buffer is now smaller (only allocated for block-path traces). Net memory is roughly unchanged.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n2. Verify correctness: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass.\n3. Measure APC 300:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc300.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc300.json apc300\n   ```\n   Check: MLE Rounds < 148ms, STARK excl trace < 1273ms.\n4. Measure APC 0:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc000.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc000.json apc000\n   ```\n   Check: No regression (STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).\n5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm `warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel` runs with reduced block count. Compare `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` instance count (should be lower — block-path only).\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms: revert.\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 10ms: revert.\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace > 2160ms): revert.\n- Any test failure: revert.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":244,"newStart":1,"newLines":260,"lines":["-# Plan: Warp-per-trace MLE batch evaluation for small traces","+# Plan: Warp-per-trace monomial MLE evaluation for small traces"," "," ## Goal"," ","-Improve MLE Rounds GPU utilization at APC 300 by introducing a warp-per-trace evaluation path for traces with small `num_y`. Currently, the batch MLE kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one or more 128-thread blocks per trace regardless of `num_y`. Traces with `num_y` ≤ 32 waste ≥75% of threads. A dedicated warp-per-trace kernel packs 4 small traces per 128-thread block, directly producing per-trace sums via warp-level reduction and eliminating the secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel for the small-trace partition.","+Improve MLE Rounds GPU utilization at APC 300 by introducing a warp-per-trace monomial evaluation path for traces with few monomials. Currently, `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` (used for `low_early` + `late_eval` traces where `num_y ≤ 512`) parallelizes over monomials with `THREADS_PER_BLOCK = 256`. Since most AIRs have only 5–20 monomials, each block has 256 threads but only 5–20 are active (2–8% utilization). The optimization introduces a warp-per-trace kernel that flips the parallelism axis: each thread handles one y-value and loops over all monomials, packing 4 traces per 128-thread block with warp-level reduction."," ","-Target: reduce MLE Rounds at APC 300 from 159ms toward ~120–135ms (saving 25–40ms), improving the APC 0→300 scaling from 0.70x toward ~0.86–0.93x.","+Target: reduce MLE Rounds at APC 300 from 159ms toward ~135–145ms (15–25ms improvement), improving scaling from 0.70x toward ~0.77–0.83x."," "," ## Current Code Path"," ","-### Zerocheck batch MLE","+### Routing: which traces reach which kernel"," ","-1. **Rust builder** (`batch_mle.rs:121–218`, `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`):","-   - Computes `threads_per_block = max_num_y.min(128)` across ALL traces (line 147).","-   - Builds `BlockCtx` array: one entry per block per trace. For a trace with `num_y = 4` and `threads_per_block = 128`, it gets 1 block where only 4/128 = 3.1% of threads are active.","-   - Allocates per-trace `ZerocheckCtx` with intermediates buffers.","-   - Uploads block_ctxs, zc_ctxs, air_offsets to device.","+In `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` (`mod.rs:1640–1946`):","+- **late_eval** traces (round > n_lift, effectively num_y=1): handled by `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` with `evaluate(1)` (line 1876) → launches `zerocheck_monomial_kernel`.","+- **low_early** traces (num_y ≤ monomial_num_y_threshold=512): handled by `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` with `evaluate(num_x)` (line 1876) → launches `zerocheck_monomial_kernel`.","+- **high_mono_traces** (num_y > 512, low monomial ratio): `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch` → launches `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel`.","+- **high_dag_traces** (num_y > 512, high monomial ratio): `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder` → launches `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`."," ","-2. **CUDA kernel** (`batch_mle.cu:109–204`, `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`):","-   - Grid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block)`.","-   - Each block reads its `BlockCtx` → gets `air_idx` and `local_block_idx_x`.","-   - Thread computes `y_int = threadIdx.x + local_block_idx_x * blockDim.x`. Guard: `active_thread = (y_int < num_y)`.","-   - Active threads evaluate the constraint DAG at `(x_int, y_int)`, multiply by `eq_xi[y_int]`, accumulate sum.","-   - `block_reduce_sum` (sumcheck.cuh:160–185): warp reduce → shared memory → first-warp reduce.","-   - Thread 0 writes to `tmp_sums_buffer[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`.","+For logup, analogous: `LogupMonomialBatch` (num_y ≤ 512), `LogupMleBatchBuilder` (num_y > 512)."," ","-3. **Secondary reduction** (`batch_mle.cu:400–410`, `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`):","-   - Grid: `(num_airs, num_x)`. Reduces per-block partial sums to per-air output.","-   - For a trace with 1 block, this is a no-op copy. Still costs one block dispatch.","+At APC 300 with 623 small AIR instances: the vast majority have num_y ≤ 512 in early rounds, and ALL traces transition to late_eval (num_y=1) as MLE rounds progress. The `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` and `LogupMonomialBatch` handle most of the work."," ","-### Logup batch MLE","+### zerocheck_monomial_kernel (`batch_mle_monomial.cu:102–151`)"," ","-Identical structure (`batch_mle.rs:268–408`, `batch_mle.cu:206–448`). The kernel computes `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt), with two sequential `block_reduce_sum` calls plus `__syncthreads` between them.","+Grid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block=256)`."," ","-### Where time is wasted at APC 300","+Block assignment (Rust, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:152–168`):","+```","+mono_blocks = ceil(num_monomials / 256)","+total_blocks = mono_blocks * num_y   // one set of mono_blocks per y-value","+```"," ","-At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances across 2 segments:","-- `max_num_y` is large (up to 2^20) → `threads_per_block = 128` always.","-- In early MLE rounds: many traces have `num_y` = 1–32 (small APC-generated AIRs, plus smaller original AIRs).","-- In later MLE rounds: `num_y` halves each round, so even originally-large traces eventually become \"small.\"","-- Each small trace gets its own 128-thread block. With 500+ small traces per launch, this produces 1500+ blocks (×3 x-points) with <25% average thread utilization.","-- The secondary reduction kernel also launches 1 block per small trace, mostly doing trivial single-element \"reductions.\"","+Kernel logic:","+- Decodes `y_int = local_block_idx_x / mono_blocks` and `mono_block = local_block_idx_x % mono_blocks`.","+- Thread `m = mono_block * 256 + threadIdx.x` evaluates monomial `m` if `m < num_monomials`.","+- Evaluates product of variables for that monomial, multiplies by `lambda_combinations[m]`.","+- `block_reduce_sum` sums across all threads → per-(y_int, x_int) partial sum.","+- Thread 0 multiplies by `eq_xi[y_int]` and writes to `tmp_sums[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`."," ","-nsight kernel profile:","-- `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`: 24 instances, 27ms total, 1.1ms avg.","-- `logup_batch_mle_kernel`: 45 instances, 21ms total, 0.46ms avg.","-- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums<true>`: 797 instances, 7.8ms total.","-- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` (logup variant via FracExt): similar.","+Secondary reduction: `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduces across mono_blocks for same (air, y_int)."," ","+**Waste at APC 300**: A typical APC AIR has `num_monomials ≈ 5–15`. With `threads_per_block = 256`:","+- `mono_blocks = 1` (ceil(15/256) = 1)","+- 256 threads per block, 15 active → 5.9% utilization","+- Per trace: `num_y` blocks of 256 threads each, totaling `num_y × 256` threads with `num_y × 15` useful FMAs","+","+nsight profile: `zerocheck_monomial_kernel`: 178 instances, 17ms total, 0.09ms avg.","+","+### LogupMonomialBatch (`batch_mle_monomial.rs:588–793`)","+","+Same pattern. `THREADS_PER_BLOCK_LOGUP = 128`. Block assignment differs slightly (accounts for both numerator and denominator interactions). Same utilization problem.","+"," ## Changes"," ","-### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace zerocheck evaluation","+### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace monomial evaluation"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.cu`"," ","-Add `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`:","+Add `warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel`:"," "," ```cuda","-__global__ void warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel(","+// Warp-per-trace: each warp handles one trace.","+// Thread = one y-value. Each thread loops over ALL monomials for its y-value.","+// Constraint: num_y <= WARP_SIZE and num_monomials <= warp_mono_limit (e.g. 32).","+__global__ void warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel(","     FpExt *__restrict__ output,           // [num_traces * num_x], direct final output","-    const ZerocheckCtx *__restrict__ d_zc_ctxs,","-    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids,  // [num_warps] maps warp → trace local index","-    const FpExt *__restrict__ d_lambda_pows,","-    size_t lambda_len,","+    const MonomialAirCtx *__restrict__ air_ctxs,","+    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids, // [num_warps] → air_ctxs index","     uint32_t num_traces"," ) {","-    uint32_t warp_id_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP_SIZE;","-    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP_SIZE;","+    constexpr uint32_t WARP = 32;","+    uint32_t warp_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP;","+    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP;","     uint32_t num_x = gridDim.y;","     uint32_t x_int = blockIdx.y;"," ","-    if (warp_id_global >= num_traces) return;","-    uint32_t local_air = trace_ids[warp_id_global];","-    ZerocheckCtx zc_ctx = d_zc_ctxs[local_air];","+    if (warp_global >= num_traces) return;","+    MonomialAirCtx actx = air_ctxs[trace_ids[warp_global]];"," ","     uint32_t y_int = lane;","-    bool active = (y_int < zc_ctx.num_y);","+    bool active = (y_int < actx.num_y);"," ","     FpExt sum(Fp::zero());","     if (active) {","-        // Same evaluation logic as existing kernel (lines 128–197 of batch_mle.cu)","-        uint32_t height = num_x * zc_ctx.num_y;","-        uint32_t row = x_int * zc_ctx.num_y + y_int;","-        // ... [DAG evaluation, eq_xi multiply — identical to existing kernel body]","+        uint32_t height = num_x * actx.num_y;","+        uint32_t row = x_int * actx.num_y + y_int;","+","+        // Loop over ALL monomials (small count, so serial is fine)","+        for (uint32_t m = 0; m < actx.num_monomials; ++m) {","+            MonomialHeader hdr = actx.d_headers[m];","+            FpExt product(Fp::one());","+            for (uint16_t v = 0; v < hdr.num_vars; ++v) {","+                PackedVar var = actx.d_variables[hdr.var_offset + v];","+                product *= eval_variable(var, row, actx.eval_ctx, height);","+            }","+            sum += product * actx.d_lambda_combinations[m];","+        }","+        sum *= actx.d_eq_xi[y_int];","     }"," ","-    // Warp-level reduction only (no shared memory, no block_reduce_sum)","+    // Warp reduction: sum across y-values in this trace","     sum = warp_reduce_sum(sum);"," ","     if (lane == 0) {","-        output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;","+        output[warp_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;","     }"," }"," ```"," ","-Key differences from existing kernel:","-- **One warp per trace** (not one block per trace). 4 warps per 128-thread block → 4 traces per block.","-- **`warp_reduce_sum` only** — no shared memory, no `__syncthreads`, no inter-warp reduction.","-- **Direct output** — writes to final `output` buffer, no `tmp_sums_buffer` or secondary reduction needed.","-- **`trace_ids` array** maps warp index → ZerocheckCtx index, allowing the same `d_zc_ctxs` array as the large-trace kernel.","+Key design choices:","+- **Parallelism axis flipped**: threads map to y-values (not monomials). Each thread serially evaluates all monomials — this is correct because `num_monomials ≤ 32`, so the serial loop is short.","+- **Direct output**: writes to final output buffer. No `tmp_sums` intermediary, no secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` launch.","+- **Warp reduction**: `warp_reduce_sum` sums across y-values within the trace. No shared memory needed.","+- **4 warps per block** (128 threads): 4 traces processed per block.","+- **`trace_ids` indirection**: maps warp index → `air_ctxs` slot, allowing the same `air_ctxs` array to serve both the warp kernel (small traces) and the existing kernel (large traces)."," ","-Add corresponding launcher `_warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle`:","-- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / warps_per_block), num_x)`, Block: `(128)` (= 4 warps).","-- `warps_per_block = 128 / WARP_SIZE = 4`.","+Add C launcher `_warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched`:","+- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / 4), num_x)`, Block: `(128)`.","+- No tmp_sums allocation, no secondary reduce launch."," ","-### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup evaluation","+### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup monomial evaluation"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.cu`"," ","-Add `warp_logup_batch_mle_kernel` — same pattern as Change 1 but with `FracExt` output (numer + denom) and two `warp_reduce_sum` calls:","+Add `warp_logup_monomial_kernel` — same warp-per-trace pattern, but accumulates both `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt output). Two `warp_reduce_sum` calls (no `__syncthreads` needed — single warp)."," ","-```cuda","-FpExt numer_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(numer_sum);","-FpExt denom_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(denom_sum);","-if (lane == 0) {","-    output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = {numer_reduced, denom_reduced};","-}","-```","+The logup monomial kernel (`logup_monomial_kernel`, `batch_mle_monomial.cu`) iterates over interactions and evaluates numerator/denominator expressions. The warp kernel maintains the same logic but with each thread covering one y-value and looping over interactions."," ","-No `__syncthreads` needed between the two reductions because they operate within a single warp.","+Add C launcher `_warp_logup_monomial_batched`."," ","-Add corresponding launcher `_warp_logup_batch_eval_mle`.","+### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings"," ","-### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings for new kernels","-"," **File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`"," "," Add extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers:","-- `warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(output, zc_ctxs, trace_ids, lambda_pows, lambda_len, num_traces, num_x)`","-- `warp_logup_batch_eval_mle(output, logup_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x)`","+- `warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched(output, air_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x) -> Result`","+- `warp_logup_monomial_batched(output, logup_air_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x) -> Result`"," ","-### Change 4: Partition logic in builders","+### Change 4: Partition in ZerocheckMonomialBatch"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`"," ","-Modify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`):","+Modify `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new`:"," "," ```rust"," const WARP_SIZE: u32 = 32;"," ","-// Partition traces into small (num_y <= WARP_SIZE) and large (num_y > WARP_SIZE)","-let (small_traces, large_traces): (Vec<&TraceCtx>, Vec<&TraceCtx>) =","-    traces.into_iter().partition(|t| t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE);","+// Partition traces: \"warp-eligible\" if num_y <= WARP_SIZE AND num_monomials <= WARP_SIZE","+let (warp_traces, block_traces): (Vec<_>, Vec<_>) = traces","+    .into_iter()","+    .enumerate()","+    .partition(|(_, t)| {","+        let nm = pk.per_air[t.air_idx].other_data.zerocheck_monomials","+            .as_ref().unwrap().num_monomials;","+        t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE && nm <= WARP_SIZE","+    });"," ```"," ","-Store both partitions in the builder. The builder gets new fields:","+New builder fields:"," ```rust","-// Existing fields (now only for large traces):","-d_block_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,","-threads_per_block: u32,","-air_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,","-// New fields (for small traces, warp-per-trace path):","-small_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,  // maps warp → zc_ctx index","-num_small_traces: u32,","+// Warp-path fields:","+warp_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,   // maps warp → air_ctxs index","+num_warp_traces: u32,","+// Block-path fields (existing):","+block_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,","+block_air_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,","+num_block_traces: u32,","+// Shared:","+air_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<MonomialAirCtx>,  // all traces, indexed by local position"," ```"," ","-The `d_zc_ctxs` / `d_logup_ctxs` arrays contain ALL traces (small + large) indexed by their local position. The large-trace path uses `BlockCtx.air_idx` to index into it; the small-trace path uses `trace_ids[warp_id_global]`.","+The `air_ctxs` array contains ALL traces (warp + block), with warp-eligible traces first. The `warp_trace_ids` maps warp index → position in `air_ctxs`. The `block_ctxs` and `block_air_offsets` only reference block-eligible traces via `BlockCtx.air_idx` pointing into the same `air_ctxs` array (with appropriate offset)."," ","-### Change 5: Two-launch evaluation in builder.evaluate()","+### Change 5: Two-launch evaluate"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`"," ","-Modify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::evaluate` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::evaluate`):","+Modify `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate`:"," "," ```rust","-pub fn evaluate(&self, lambda_pows: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {","-    let total_traces = self.traces.len();","-    if total_traces == 0 {","-        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());","-    }","+pub fn evaluate(&self, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {","+    let total = self.traces.len();","+    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total * num_x as usize);"," ","-    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_traces * num_x as usize);","-","-    // Launch 1: Small traces via warp-per-trace kernel (direct output, no tmp buffer)","-    if self.num_small_traces > 0 {","+    // Launch 1: warp-per-trace for small traces → writes to output[0..num_warp * num_x]","+    if self.num_warp_traces > 0 {","         unsafe {","-            warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(","-                &mut output,  // writes to output[0..num_small * num_x]","-                &self.d_zc_ctxs,","-                &self.small_trace_ids,","-                lambda_pows,","-                lambda_pows.len(),","-                self.num_small_traces,","-                num_x,","+            warp_zerocheck_monomial_batched(","+                &mut output, &self.air_ctxs, &self.warp_trace_ids,","+                self.num_warp_traces, num_x,","             )?;","         }","     }"," ","-    // Launch 2: Large traces via existing block-per-trace kernel","-    if !self.d_block_ctxs.is_empty() {","-        let num_large = self.d_block_ctxs.len(); // blocks, not traces","-        let num_large_airs = self.large_trace_count;","-        // tmp_sums_buffer and secondary reduce, writing to output[num_small * num_x..]","-        // ...existing code with offset into output buffer...","+    // Launch 2: existing block kernel for remaining traces","+    if self.num_block_traces > 0 {","+        let offset = self.num_warp_traces as usize * num_x as usize;","+        // Allocate tmp_sums for block path","+        let num_blocks = self.block_ctxs.len();","+        let mut tmp_sums = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_blocks * num_x as usize);","+        unsafe {","+            zerocheck_monomial_batched(","+                &mut tmp_sums,","+                output.as_mut_ptr().add(offset), // write after warp output","+                &self.block_ctxs, &self.air_ctxs,","+                &self.block_air_offsets,","+                num_blocks as u32, num_x,","+                self.num_block_traces, THREADS_PER_BLOCK,","+            )?;","+        }","     }"," ","     Ok(output)"," }"," ```"," ","-The output buffer layout: `[small_trace_0_x0, small_trace_0_x1, ..., small_trace_N_x0, ..., large_trace_0_x0, ...]`. The caller reads by trace index, so we maintain a mapping from original trace index → output position. This is handled by ordering the `traces` Vec consistently: small traces first, then large traces.","+The output layout: warp-eligible traces first (contiguous), then block-eligible traces. `trace_indices()` returns indices in the same order (warp first, block second)."," ","-### Change 6: Adjust callers to handle reordered output","+### Change 6: Apply same partition to LogupMonomialBatch"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`"," ","-The `trace_indices()` / `trace_info()` iterators already return trace indices in the order they were stored. With the partition, the builder stores small traces first, then large traces. Callers iterate over `trace_indices()` and use the index to write into the correct `zc_out[trace_idx]` or `logup_out[trace_idx]`, so no caller changes are needed as long as the builder maintains the trace-index-to-output-position mapping correctly.","+Same pattern for `LogupMonomialBatch::new` and `evaluate`, using `warp_logup_monomial_batched`. The logup monomial kernel has `THREADS_PER_BLOCK_LOGUP = 128`, similar underutilization."," "," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Correctness**: The warp-per-trace kernel must produce identical per-trace sums as the existing block-per-trace kernel. Both evaluate the same DAG over the same data and reduce to a single sum per trace per x-evaluation-point. The warp-level reduction is exact (no atomics, no floating-point reordering) because it uses `warp_reduce_sum` which is the same function used in the first stage of `block_reduce_sum`.","+1. **Correctness**: The warp kernel evaluates the same monomials × lambda_combinations for the same (x_int, y_int) pairs, producing the same per-trace sums. The serial monomial loop in the warp kernel is mathematically equivalent to the parallel-monomial approach in the block kernel, because addition is commutative and associative over the field. `warp_reduce_sum` over y-values produces the same result as the block kernel's `block_reduce_sum` over mono-blocks followed by `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` over (y_int × mono_blocks)."," ","-2. **Threshold correctness**: Traces with `num_y > WARP_SIZE` must NOT use the warp path (they'd lose data — lane_id only covers 0..31). The partition at `num_y <= 32` guarantees all active threads fit in one warp.","+2. **Threshold safety**: Only traces with `num_y <= WARP_SIZE=32` AND `num_monomials <= WARP_SIZE=32` use the warp path. The `num_y` guard ensures no y-values are missed (thread with `lane >= num_y` contributes zero). The `num_monomials` bound ensures the serial loop is short enough that per-thread work doesn't become a bottleneck."," ","-3. **Intermediates buffers**: Small traces with `buffer_size > 0` still need intermediates. The intermediates allocation uses `height = num_x * num_y`, which is correct regardless of kernel type. The `buffer_stride = height` in the kernel is also correct.","+3. **No intermediates needed**: The monomial kernel does NOT use intermediates buffers (unlike the DAG batch kernel). Each monomial is evaluated independently from trace data. This means the warp kernel needs no intermediates allocation at all."," ","-4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large heights → most go to the existing block-per-trace path. The small-trace partition may have 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead from the extra kernel launch.","+4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large num_y (>32) → they go to the block kernel path. The warp partition has 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead."," ","-5. **Memory**: The `small_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_small_traces * 4 bytes` (~2KB at APC 300). The output buffer size is unchanged (one entry per trace per x-point). The `tmp_sums_buffer` is only allocated for large traces, so its size decreases.","+5. **Memory**: The `warp_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_warp_traces * 4` bytes (≈2KB). The `tmp_sums` buffer is now smaller (only allocated for block-path traces). Net memory is roughly unchanged."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," "," 1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`","-2. Verify correctness: Run `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass.","+2. Verify correctness: `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass."," 3. Measure APC 300:","    ```bash","    RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc300.json --recursion","    python3 spec.py current_apc300.json apc300","    ```","-   Check: MLE Rounds < 135ms, STARK excl trace < 1260ms.","+   Check: MLE Rounds < 148ms, STARK excl trace < 1273ms."," 4. Measure APC 0:","    ```bash","    RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc000.json --recursion","    python3 spec.py current_apc000.json apc000","    ```","-   Check: No regression (MLE Rounds ≤ 115ms, STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).","-5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm reduced block count and improved kernel time for `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`.","+   Check: No regression (STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).","+5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm `warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel` runs with reduced block count. Compare `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` instance count (should be lower — block-path only)."," "," ## Rollback Criteria"," ","-- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below noise threshold): revert.","-- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 15ms: revert.","+- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms: revert.","+- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 10ms: revert."," - Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace > 2160ms): revert."," - Any test failure: revert."]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Warp-per-trace MLE batch evaluation for small traces\n\n## Goal\n\nImprove MLE Rounds GPU utilization at APC 300 by introducing a warp-per-trace evaluation path for traces with small `num_y`. Currently, the batch MLE kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) assign one or more 128-thread blocks per trace regardless of `num_y`. Traces with `num_y` ≤ 32 waste ≥75% of threads. A dedicated warp-per-trace kernel packs 4 small traces per 128-thread block, directly producing per-trace sums via warp-level reduction and eliminating the secondary `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel for the small-trace partition.\n\nTarget: reduce MLE Rounds at APC 300 from 159ms toward ~120–135ms (saving 25–40ms), improving the APC 0→300 scaling from 0.70x toward ~0.86–0.93x.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Zerocheck batch MLE\n\n1. **Rust builder** (`batch_mle.rs:121–218`, `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n   - Computes `threads_per_block = max_num_y.min(128)` across ALL traces (line 147).\n   - Builds `BlockCtx` array: one entry per block per trace. For a trace with `num_y = 4` and `threads_per_block = 128`, it gets 1 block where only 4/128 = 3.1% of threads are active.\n   - Allocates per-trace `ZerocheckCtx` with intermediates buffers.\n   - Uploads block_ctxs, zc_ctxs, air_offsets to device.\n\n2. **CUDA kernel** (`batch_mle.cu:109–204`, `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_blocks, num_x)`. Block: `(threads_per_block)`.\n   - Each block reads its `BlockCtx` → gets `air_idx` and `local_block_idx_x`.\n   - Thread computes `y_int = threadIdx.x + local_block_idx_x * blockDim.x`. Guard: `active_thread = (y_int < num_y)`.\n   - Active threads evaluate the constraint DAG at `(x_int, y_int)`, multiply by `eq_xi[y_int]`, accumulate sum.\n   - `block_reduce_sum` (sumcheck.cuh:160–185): warp reduce → shared memory → first-warp reduce.\n   - Thread 0 writes to `tmp_sums_buffer[blockIdx.x * num_x + x_int]`.\n\n3. **Secondary reduction** (`batch_mle.cu:400–410`, `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`):\n   - Grid: `(num_airs, num_x)`. Reduces per-block partial sums to per-air output.\n   - For a trace with 1 block, this is a no-op copy. Still costs one block dispatch.\n\n### Logup batch MLE\n\nIdentical structure (`batch_mle.rs:268–408`, `batch_mle.cu:206–448`). The kernel computes `numer_sum` and `denom_sum` (FracExt), with two sequential `block_reduce_sum` calls plus `__syncthreads` between them.\n\n### Where time is wasted at APC 300\n\nAt APC 300 with 623 AIR instances across 2 segments:\n- `max_num_y` is large (up to 2^20) → `threads_per_block = 128` always.\n- In early MLE rounds: many traces have `num_y` = 1–32 (small APC-generated AIRs, plus smaller original AIRs).\n- In later MLE rounds: `num_y` halves each round, so even originally-large traces eventually become \"small.\"\n- Each small trace gets its own 128-thread block. With 500+ small traces per launch, this produces 1500+ blocks (×3 x-points) with <25% average thread utilization.\n- The secondary reduction kernel also launches 1 block per small trace, mostly doing trivial single-element \"reductions.\"\n\nnsight kernel profile:\n- `zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`: 24 instances, 27ms total, 1.1ms avg.\n- `logup_batch_mle_kernel`: 45 instances, 21ms total, 0.46ms avg.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums<true>`: 797 instances, 7.8ms total.\n- `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` (logup variant via FracExt): similar.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace zerocheck evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel(\n    FpExt *__restrict__ output,           // [num_traces * num_x], direct final output\n    const ZerocheckCtx *__restrict__ d_zc_ctxs,\n    const uint32_t *__restrict__ trace_ids,  // [num_warps] maps warp → trace local index\n    const FpExt *__restrict__ d_lambda_pows,\n    size_t lambda_len,\n    uint32_t num_traces\n) {\n    uint32_t warp_id_global = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t lane = threadIdx.x % WARP_SIZE;\n    uint32_t num_x = gridDim.y;\n    uint32_t x_int = blockIdx.y;\n\n    if (warp_id_global >= num_traces) return;\n    uint32_t local_air = trace_ids[warp_id_global];\n    ZerocheckCtx zc_ctx = d_zc_ctxs[local_air];\n\n    uint32_t y_int = lane;\n    bool active = (y_int < zc_ctx.num_y);\n\n    FpExt sum(Fp::zero());\n    if (active) {\n        // Same evaluation logic as existing kernel (lines 128–197 of batch_mle.cu)\n        uint32_t height = num_x * zc_ctx.num_y;\n        uint32_t row = x_int * zc_ctx.num_y + y_int;\n        // ... [DAG evaluation, eq_xi multiply — identical to existing kernel body]\n    }\n\n    // Warp-level reduction only (no shared memory, no block_reduce_sum)\n    sum = warp_reduce_sum(sum);\n\n    if (lane == 0) {\n        output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey differences from existing kernel:\n- **One warp per trace** (not one block per trace). 4 warps per 128-thread block → 4 traces per block.\n- **`warp_reduce_sum` only** — no shared memory, no `__syncthreads`, no inter-warp reduction.\n- **Direct output** — writes to final `output` buffer, no `tmp_sums_buffer` or secondary reduction needed.\n- **`trace_ids` array** maps warp index → ZerocheckCtx index, allowing the same `d_zc_ctxs` array as the large-trace kernel.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle`:\n- Grid: `(ceil(num_traces / warps_per_block), num_x)`, Block: `(128)` (= 4 warps).\n- `warps_per_block = 128 / WARP_SIZE = 4`.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace logup evaluation\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.cu`\n\nAdd `warp_logup_batch_mle_kernel` — same pattern as Change 1 but with `FracExt` output (numer + denom) and two `warp_reduce_sum` calls:\n\n```cuda\nFpExt numer_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(numer_sum);\nFpExt denom_reduced = warp_reduce_sum(denom_sum);\nif (lane == 0) {\n    output[warp_id_global * num_x + x_int] = {numer_reduced, denom_reduced};\n}\n```\n\nNo `__syncthreads` needed between the two reductions because they operate within a single warp.\n\nAdd corresponding launcher `_warp_logup_batch_eval_mle`.\n\n### Change 3: Rust FFI bindings for new kernels\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers:\n- `warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(output, zc_ctxs, trace_ids, lambda_pows, lambda_len, num_traces, num_x)`\n- `warp_logup_batch_eval_mle(output, logup_ctxs, trace_ids, num_traces, num_x)`\n\n### Change 4: Partition logic in builders\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`):\n\n```rust\nconst WARP_SIZE: u32 = 32;\n\n// Partition traces into small (num_y <= WARP_SIZE) and large (num_y > WARP_SIZE)\nlet (small_traces, large_traces): (Vec<&TraceCtx>, Vec<&TraceCtx>) =\n    traces.into_iter().partition(|t| t.num_y <= WARP_SIZE);\n```\n\nStore both partitions in the builder. The builder gets new fields:\n```rust\n// Existing fields (now only for large traces):\nd_block_ctxs: DeviceBuffer<BlockCtx>,\nthreads_per_block: u32,\nair_offsets: DeviceBuffer<u32>,\n// New fields (for small traces, warp-per-trace path):\nsmall_trace_ids: DeviceBuffer<u32>,  // maps warp → zc_ctx index\nnum_small_traces: u32,\n```\n\nThe `d_zc_ctxs` / `d_logup_ctxs` arrays contain ALL traces (small + large) indexed by their local position. The large-trace path uses `BlockCtx.air_idx` to index into it; the small-trace path uses `trace_ids[warp_id_global]`.\n\n### Change 5: Two-launch evaluation in builder.evaluate()\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nModify `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::evaluate` (and analogously `LogupMleBatchBuilder::evaluate`):\n\n```rust\npub fn evaluate(&self, lambda_pows: &DeviceBuffer<EF>, num_x: u32) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, KernelError> {\n    let total_traces = self.traces.len();\n    if total_traces == 0 {\n        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n    }\n\n    let mut output = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(total_traces * num_x as usize);\n\n    // Launch 1: Small traces via warp-per-trace kernel (direct output, no tmp buffer)\n    if self.num_small_traces > 0 {\n        unsafe {\n            warp_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle(\n                &mut output,  // writes to output[0..num_small * num_x]\n                &self.d_zc_ctxs,\n                &self.small_trace_ids,\n                lambda_pows,\n                lambda_pows.len(),\n                self.num_small_traces,\n                num_x,\n            )?;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Launch 2: Large traces via existing block-per-trace kernel\n    if !self.d_block_ctxs.is_empty() {\n        let num_large = self.d_block_ctxs.len(); // blocks, not traces\n        let num_large_airs = self.large_trace_count;\n        // tmp_sums_buffer and secondary reduce, writing to output[num_small * num_x..]\n        // ...existing code with offset into output buffer...\n    }\n\n    Ok(output)\n}\n```\n\nThe output buffer layout: `[small_trace_0_x0, small_trace_0_x1, ..., small_trace_N_x0, ..., large_trace_0_x0, ...]`. The caller reads by trace index, so we maintain a mapping from original trace index → output position. This is handled by ordering the `traces` Vec consistently: small traces first, then large traces.\n\n### Change 6: Adjust callers to handle reordered output\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nThe `trace_indices()` / `trace_info()` iterators already return trace indices in the order they were stored. With the partition, the builder stores small traces first, then large traces. Callers iterate over `trace_indices()` and use the index to write into the correct `zc_out[trace_idx]` or `logup_out[trace_idx]`, so no caller changes are needed as long as the builder maintains the trace-index-to-output-position mapping correctly.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The warp-per-trace kernel must produce identical per-trace sums as the existing block-per-trace kernel. Both evaluate the same DAG over the same data and reduce to a single sum per trace per x-evaluation-point. The warp-level reduction is exact (no atomics, no floating-point reordering) because it uses `warp_reduce_sum` which is the same function used in the first stage of `block_reduce_sum`.\n\n2. **Threshold correctness**: Traces with `num_y > WARP_SIZE` must NOT use the warp path (they'd lose data — lane_id only covers 0..31). The partition at `num_y <= 32` guarantees all active threads fit in one warp.\n\n3. **Intermediates buffers**: Small traces with `buffer_size > 0` still need intermediates. The intermediates allocation uses `height = num_x * num_y`, which is correct regardless of kernel type. The `buffer_stride = height` in the kernel is also correct.\n\n4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, few segments), most traces have large heights → most go to the existing block-per-trace path. The small-trace partition may have 0–5 traces, producing negligible overhead from the extra kernel launch.\n\n5. **Memory**: The `small_trace_ids` buffer is at most `num_small_traces * 4 bytes` (~2KB at APC 300). The output buffer size is unchanged (one entry per trace per x-point). The `tmp_sums_buffer` is only allocated for large traces, so its size decreases.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n2. Verify correctness: Run `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` — all tests must pass.\n3. Measure APC 300:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc300.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc300.json apc300\n   ```\n   Check: MLE Rounds < 135ms, STARK excl trace < 1260ms.\n4. Measure APC 0:\n   ```bash\n   RUST_LOG=info powdr_openvm_riscv prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics current_apc000.json --recursion\n   python3 spec.py current_apc000.json apc000\n   ```\n   Check: No regression (MLE Rounds ≤ 115ms, STARK excl trace ≤ 2150ms).\n5. Profile APC 300 with nsight: confirm reduced block count and improved kernel time for `warp_zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below noise threshold): revert.\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves by < 15ms: revert.\n- Any regression at APC 0 (STARK excl trace > 2160ms): revert.\n- Any test failure: revert.\n"}}
